WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.925
Command of Evidence: Textual

00:00:03.305 --> 00:00:07.327
Split the claim into parts. Evidence must hit every part.

00:00:08.527 --> 00:00:09.526
How to spot it

00:00:09.926 --> 00:00:11.525
A claim or hypothesis

00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:15.164
The question says “most directly support” or “weaken”

00:00:15.714 --> 00:00:18.876
It’s testing: what actually counts as evidence

00:00:20.076 --> 00:00:20.871
The method

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:23.929
Step 1: Find the exact claim

00:00:24.209 --> 00:00:27.312
Usually the last sentence, or the hypothesis.

00:00:27.862 --> 00:00:30.089
Step 2: Split it into parts

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.834
Toe pads + bigger + after the storm.

00:00:33.384 --> 00:00:35.736
Step 3: Support or weaken?

00:00:36.016 --> 00:00:37.990
Decide before reading the choices.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:40.609
Step 4: Hit every part

00:00:40.889 --> 00:00:42.473
The right topic isn’t enough.

00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:44.133
In a few words

00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:46.454
Claim. Parts. Direction.

00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:48.796
Evidence rules

00:00:49.196 --> 00:00:51.883
Support = the predicted result happens

00:00:52.163 --> 00:00:53.698
Makes the claim more likely.

00:00:54.248 --> 00:00:57.027
Weaken = same result, different cause

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.063
Or the cause without the result.

00:00:59.613 --> 00:01:01.253
Hit every part of the claim

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.331
Half the claim is a wrong answer.

00:01:03.881 --> 00:01:05.868
Quotes: literal meaning wins

00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:07.711
Pretty language doesn’t count.

00:01:08.911 --> 00:01:09.951
Let’s try one

00:01:10.363 --> 00:01:17.201
Ecologist Amara Osei hypothesizes that a species of lizard on a small island has developed larger

00:01:17.201 --> 00:01:24.903
toe pads since a hurricane struck the island in 2017, because lizards with larger pads were better

00:01:24.903 --> 00:01:28.231
able to cling to branches during the storm’s high winds.

00:01:28.781 --> 00:01:33.781
Which finding, if true, would most directly support Osei’s hypothesis?

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:40.428
The claim: the lizards developed larger toe pads since the 2017 hurricane.

00:01:40.428 --> 00:01:45.429
Two parts: bigger toe pads, and a change after the storm.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:51.757
Diet, other species, wind speed: none of them show that these lizards changed.

00:01:52.307 --> 00:01:57.368
B shows bigger toe pads after the hurricane. That covers both parts.

00:01:58.568 --> 00:01:59.563
Common traps

00:01:59.963 --> 00:02:01.703
Right topic, wrong claim

00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:03.919
About the subject, not the claim.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:05.510
Wrong direction

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.332
Supports when you need weaken.

00:02:08.532 --> 00:02:10.857
Command of Evidence: Textual

00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:13.309
Claim. Parts. Direction.

00:02:13.859 --> 00:02:14.632
Spot it

00:02:14.832 --> 00:02:17.950
A claim + “most directly support / weaken”

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.207
Steps

00:02:19.407 --> 00:02:21.437
1. Find the exact claim

00:02:21.717 --> 00:02:23.563
2. Split it into parts

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.558
3. Support or weaken?

00:02:25.838 --> 00:02:28.340
4. Pick the one that hits every part

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:29.642
Remember

00:02:29.842 --> 00:02:32.529
Support = the predicted result happens

00:02:32.809 --> 00:02:35.765
Weaken = no result, or a different cause

00:02:36.045 --> 00:02:37.526
Half the claim = wrong

00:02:37.806 --> 00:02:39.989
Quotes: the literal meaning wins

00:02:40.539 --> 00:02:41.144
Trap

00:02:41.344 --> 00:02:43.085
Right topic, wrong claim

00:02:43.835 --> 00:02:47.529
